  prevention  reinforcing the use of exercise in  addition to current therapeutic  strategies  so what actually is cancer well cancer  is best thought as  uncontrollable cell growth basically a  cell that just keeps on dividing even  when it shouldn't  that can result in the formation of  tumours a good way of understanding  cancer  are through the different hallmarks of  cancer described by hannah hannah and  weinberg  and if you want me to make a video on  that do let me know because  i have been considering it for a while  anyway the reason i bring  the hallmarks up is because it's a  useful framework to use to understand  how exercise  might be implicating the different  hallmarks of cancer  the hallmark that we're going to focus  on in this video is a fading immune  destruction  and effectively this describes how  cancer cells  are able to avoid getting destroyed and  removed and killed  by different immune cells within your  body and one way that it's thought that  tumors can evade immune destruction  is by actually suppressing the activity  of different immune cells  and so you may have heard of  immunotherapy which is effectively a  therapy that can block  different receptors to stop the tumor  cells from inhibiting these different  immune cells  and so the reason i bring this up is  because there's evidence to support that  the anti-tumor benefits of exercise  could be mediated through while at least  partially mediated through the immune  response  key players in this immune response are  the natural killer cells  which as you can guess by their name  they can recognize and kill  tumor cells and so interestingly a study  that came out in 2016  showed that tumor-bearing mice that  underwent a six-week  wheel-running intervention actually  induced the mobilization of these  natural killer cells  and increased their infiltration into  the tumours and this infiltration helps  to control  tumor growth but natural killer cells  may not be the only immune cells that  are influenced by exercise  if we now take a look at this recent  elife paper  they looked at how cytotoxic t cells may  be able to mediate  exercise-induced reductions in tumor  growth and so  cytotoxic t-cells which i may otherwise  refer to as  cd8 plus t cells are kind of analogous  to natural killer cells  in that they can identify and eliminate  tumor cells by immunosurveillance  so in this e-life study they had a  tumor-forming mouse model  whereby they had injected mice with  amirine mammary cancer cell line  that induces tumor growth and before and  after this injection  the mice were given access to an  exercise will  on which they typically run around six  kilometers per day  in addition to reducing the tumor volume  compared to non-running controls  the exercise also increased the numbers  of cd8 plus t  cells these cytotoxic t cells that  infiltrated the tumor  and when the authors depleted these cd8  plus t cells  it also reduced the beneficial effects  of exercise  seen on tumor growth and long-term  survival but if we're ever going to be  able to develop  treatment strategies through this  understanding of exercise  benefits on tumor growth then we have to  have a better understanding of how  exactly  are these cytotoxic t cells being  potentially activated  and then recruited into the tumor site  well to understand this better the  authors then looked at the metabolic  profile of plasma after exercise and  what they observed were higher levels  of krebs cycle intermediates such as  citric acid  malic acid and alpha-ketoglutarate  and the other key metabolite being  lactate that rises very rapidly after  exercise  and so the authors then looks at how  these different metabolites can actually  affect the cd8 t  cells and so what you can see in this  figure is that malate succinate  fumarate lactate and alpha ketoglutarate  all increased the levels of the cd8 t  cells that had a low expression of a  receptor known as cd62l  now this receptor is an adhesion  receptor and so having low levels of  that  receptor is associated with greater  migration of these cd8 t  cells suggesting that these metabolites  could potentially be reducing the level  of this receptor  enabling the cd8 cells to circulate and  head towards the tumor  and strikingly lactate was shown to  greatly increase the amount of grand  sign b  and icos expression on these cd8 t  cells and these are both associated with  activated  cd8 plus t cells and help in the killing  of the target cells  and so one of the cool things that they  then did is take cd8 plus t  cells from trained mice that had been  exercising and transferred them to a  tumor-forming mouse model  to assess whether or not these cd8 plus  t-cells that have been trained by  exercise  could help reduce tumor growth and as  you can see in this figure here  mice that were given t cells from the  exercise mice had a better survival rate  than those given t  cells from non-exercising mice okay so  it looks like these cd8 t  cells are showing some impact on tumor  growth but is there an alternative  strategy besides injecting t-cells  that could also possess a similar effect  well that's exactly what they tested  next  mice were given daily doses of 2 grams  per kilogram of sodium lactate  and this was administered for 12 days  before they then inoculated the mice  with some breast cancer cells  after which they then continued this  daily injection of  sodium lactate and as you can see here  as with the exercise  this in this daily injection of sodium  lactate  also reduced the tumor volume and  improve survival rates  so in sum this paper seems to show that  exercise through modulating the  metabolome can result in  the activation of cytotoxic t cells by  decreasing the expression of an adhesion  molecule  and increasing the expression of their  effector proteins  which is likely why there seems to be a  reduction in tumor growth and improved  survival rates  seen in the various different mouse  models they've used here so this data is  interesting but it also raises a number  of questions  firstly can this information be  extrapolated to humans  and is the effect of exercise mediated  through the same  manner secondly how does the type of  exercise influence this result  for example would strength exercise have  a different impact  and lastly would exercise have a similar  benefit  for pediatric cancers and another thing  i thought was worth mentioning is that  this work still doesn't fully understand  the full links between the changes in  the metabolism  and the changes in the expression  profile of these  cytotoxic t cells and so i think it's  particularly interesting  especially with their changes seen in  lactate that lactate was recently shown  to be an epigenetic regulator  and so that could explain these changes  and also alpha ketoglutarate is also  known to influence  epigenetic marks and so those two in  addition to potentially the other  metabolites  could explain how these cytotoxic t  cells are being activated  and so i think it'll be particularly  interesting to see  further information about this and  another interesting fact is that there's  a lot of lactate and yoghurt  anyway the last thing i want to just  briefly mention is  a lot of you have suggested in the  comments that i start a patreon  account and i've been thinking about  this a lot and i  think it could potentially be a nice  idea if i  managed to work out how to upload these  svg files that effectively  share all my drawings for for the  different videos  and so yeah let me know if that's  something of interest and i can properly  set it up  in addition to just having the standard  patreon  anyway i hope you found this study  interesting at least i did  and i hope you've learned something and  as always thanks for listening
 

